findings.body:
`Skeleton` is imported, but the Suspense boundary has no `fallback`:

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/flows/flow-icon-text/flow-icon-text.tsx" lines="1-18"}

::callout{type="warning"}
`ui-v2/biome.json` sets `correctness.noUnusedImports` to `"error"`, so `biome check` / CI lint will fail on this file.

There's a user-facing effect too. While the flow is loading, the component renders nothing, so the layout jumps when the name arrives. `FlowLink` and `DeploymentLink` both show a Skeleton here, and the PR description says this component has a "Suspense wrapper and Skeleton fallback".

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/flows/flow-icon-text/flow-icon-text.tsx" lines="14-16"}
		<Suspense fallback={<Skeleton className="h-4 w-full" />}>
			<FlowIconTextImplementation flowId={flowId} />
		</Suspense>

Compare with ::file{file="ui-v2/src/components/flows/flow-link.tsx"}.

The description lists `flow-icon-text.test.tsx` ("Unit tests using MSW for API mocking") and ticks the "includes unit tests" box. The `flow-icon-text/` directory only contains the component, the story, and `index.ts`, so nothing tests the new component.

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/flows/flow-icon-text/flow-icon-text.tsx" lines="20-33"}

A test should check that the flow name renders and that the link `href` is `/flows/flow/<id>`. If the fallback is restored, it should also check that the Skeleton shows while the query is pending. `deployment-link.test.tsx` already sets up the router and QueryClient and can be used as a template.
